  "narrative": "All twelve discovery results are valid independent-cell Welch comparisons with adequate cell sizes, but none provides sufficiently precise evidence of a scientifically meaningful effect exceeding 0.15 signed units in its claimed direction. The three named-marker main effects are small: H1 is essentially null, while H2 and H3 have small positive signed estimates of +0.021 and +0.035, with upper bounds well below 0.15. Therefore I reject the claimed marker-main effects and close those investigations. The marker-by-marker interactions H4 and H5 are also not supported: H4 has a signed estimate against the claimed direction, and H5 is essentially zero; both intervals fall short of the meaningfulness threshold in the claimed direction. These direct tests make marker-marker amplification unlikely for the registered comparisons, so I reject them and close those specific hypotheses. \n\nThe signature-interaction claims provide only imprecise evidence. H6 and H7 have positive signed estimates (+0.060 and +0.065), and H11 has +0.085, suggesting possible effect enhancement, but their intervals are wide and include effects opposite the claim as well as effects above 0.15. These three remain unresolved. H8 is close to null in the opposite direction and its interval does not reach 0.15, so H8 is rejected. The feature_024 comparisons are also not meaningful: H10 is near zero, and H9's signed interaction estimate is against the claimed direction with an upper bound below the threshold, so both are rejected. \n\nThe strongest candidate is H12, feature_031 positive versus negative, with a signed mean difference of +0.107 and a 95% interval of +0.035 to +0.178. This supports the hypothesized direction and excludes a null effect at conventional discovery confidence, but the point estimate is below the 0.15-unit meaningfulness threshold and the interval straddles that threshold. Given the twelvefold screening, I do not accept H12 in the discovery dataset. Instead, I keep it active and request independent validation as the sole new validation target. The validation interval will clarify whether this near-threshold association is reproducible and whether its true magnitude plausibly exceeds the scientific threshold. \n\nMethodologically, there is no evident post-outcome leakage: the compared features are measured predictors and the outcome is a separate composite score. All cells exceed the minimum size, and the nominal labels are not treated as ordered. The remaining uncertainty is primarily statistical and interpretive: these are dataset-specific associations without covariate adjustment or causal interpretation. The validation request is therefore aimed at generalizability of the association, not at establishing a biological mechanism. \n\nFollowing validation, I would prioritize feature_031 if it replicates with an effect clearly above 0.15; if validation falls well below threshold, I would close H12 and shift attention to other measured predictors rather than continue weak signature or marker-marker interactions. H6, H7, and H11 are deferred rather than closed because their intervals still permit a meaningful positive interaction, but their low priority follows from the absence of strong marker main effects.",
  "research_notes": "Discovery map: named binary marker main effects feature_007/058/015 do not support a meaningful positive-versus-negative association with stronger composite dependency; upper signed bounds are far below 0.15. Marker-marker and feature_024-based interactions are not meaningfully negative as claimed and are closed for those directions. Signature interactions H6/H7/H11 are imprecise but not excluded; defer. H12 feature_031 is the leading candidate: signed effect +0.107, CI +0.035 to +0.178, direction supported but threshold uncertain. Request independent validation of H12. If validation is near threshold or above, follow up with feature_031 subgroup/interaction analyses; if below, close feature_031 and pivot to other measured predictors rather than weak signature interactions."
